{
  "abstract": "DICTO's paper \"Fair Dice — The Aequi: Enumerating the Innumerable\" (Zenodo, 2026) proves, as its Theorem A (a), that a set of N distinct face directions, not all in one closed hemisphere, carries a die that is fair about the origin (every face seen from the origin under the solid angle 4 pi / N) exactly when the directions satisfy Alexandrov's condition for equal masses, and that the die is then unique up to scale. The proof passes to the polar polytope, whose corners lie on the face directions; the paper's Lemma 1 shows that the solid angle of a face equals the integral Gauss curvature of the polar polytope at the matching corner, so Theorem A (a) is Alexandrov's theorem on prescribed integral curvature (Alexandrov 1942, in the polytope case proved by Oliker 2007 and Bertrand 2016) for equal curvatures. This entry proves in Lean the polyhedral Alexandrov theorem with the condition stated over sets of rays (existence if and only if the curvatures sum to 4 pi and Alexandrov's condition holds for every nonempty proper set of the rays; uniqueness up to scaling about the origin); the equivalence of this form with the textbook form over spherically convex sets is not formalized. Curvature and solid angle are each defined as three times a volume inside the unit ball (the normal cone, or the cone through a face), which equals the corresponding area on the unit sphere. The entry also proves Lemma 1 (the polar identity) and Theorem A (a) (existence and uniqueness of the fair die). Existence follows Oliker's variational route: a maximizer of his functional exists by continuity and coercivity, and at a maximizer every corner has the prescribed curvature. Uniqueness is by the maximum principle. Not formalized: Theorem A (b) (the symmetry group of the die) and (c) (the centre of the die as its centroid; the Lean statements use the origin, and the centroid is not formalized), the finite test for equal masses, and the rest of the paper (the enumeration). Audience: researchers on convex polytopes, the Minkowski and Alexandrov problems and fair dice, and Mathlib contributors.",
